{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the Crusader state of Tripoli. He was born around 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father's assassination. As he was still a minor his mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. \n\nRaymond III was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1164 during the Battle of Harim and was held prisoner for nearly a decade until he was ransomed in 1173. During his captivity *Regency in Antioch* the Principality of Antioch was governed by his distant relative Bohemond III with the help of the Patriarch of Antioch Amalric of Limoges. Raymond III's experience in captivity had a profound effect on him and he became determined to make the most of his freedom.\n\nAfter his release Raymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a strong supporter of his cousin Countess Sibylla of Tripoli and her husband William of Montferrat. In 1174 he supported the accession of his cousin Baldwin IV of Jerusalem to the throne. *Later Life and Death* Raymond III died in 1187 without an heir and the County of Tripoli passed to his godson Raymond IV son of Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond III was known for his military prowess and his diplomatic skills and was considered one of the most powerful nobles in the Crusader states. He was also known for his loyalty to his family and his commitment to the Crusader cause. Throughout his life Raymond III was involved in numerous battles and political intrigues and played a significant role in shaping the history of the Crusader states. His legacy continued to be felt for many years after his death.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.011065074242651463}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.2649740